{"records":[{"id":"5ttpj7v49h","title":"Estrogen-related Hormones Induce Apoptosis by Stabilizing Schlafen-12 Protein Turnover","description":" We report here that human 17-β-estradiol (E2) and its related steroid hormones induce apoptosis by binding directly to phosphodiesterase 3A, which in turn recruits and stabilizes an otherwise fast-turnover protein Schlafen 12 (SLFN12). The elevated SLFN12 binds to ribosomes to exclude the recruitment of signal recognition particles (SRPs), thereby blocking the continuous protein translation occurring on the endoplasmic reticulum of E2-treated cells. These proteins include Bcl-2 and Mcl-1, whose ensuing decrease triggers apoptosis. The SLFN12 protein and an apoptosis activation marker were co-localized in syncytiotrophoblast of human placentas, where levels of estrogen-related hormones are high, and dynamic cell turnover by apoptosis is critical for successful implantation and placenta development.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-08-14T03:00:49.093Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5ttpj7v49h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5ttpj7v49h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dianrong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9bccjb75wp","title":"Large-Scale Analysis of Cancer Cell Fractional Killing","description":"In the clinic, few drugs are employed as single agents and it is therefore of interest to understand the impact of drug interactions specifically on fractional killing. Our ability to monitor fractional killing in many populations in parallel provided a means to search for modulators of this phenomenon more systematically. As proof-of-principle, we examined the effect on pimasertib (Pim, 5 µM)-induced cell death of 261 structurally and functionally distinct compounds in A549^N cells. The ability of all 261 compounds to modulate Pim-induced cell death was computed using an analytic framework based on the Bliss model of drug interactions. \n\nThe files below contain the raw live (mKate2 positive), dead (SYTOX Green positive) and overlapping (mKate2 positive/SYTOX Green double positive; treated as dead cells in the computation of lethal fraction scores) counts, along with the computed lethal fraction scores, for each compound. Library wells denoted as 'Empty' contained DMSO and function as controls in the DMSO treatment or pimasertib treatment conditions.\n\nNote that each screen was repeated three independent times (reps 1,2,3). Time elapsed is in hours. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-09T16:29:29.144Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9bccjb75wp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9bccjb75wp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Dixon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Cell Biology","Systems Biology","Chemotherapy","Cancer Systems Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zvnw69chcz","title":"New insights into the Manila clam – Perkinsus olseni interaction based on gene expression analysis of clam hemocytes and parasite trophozoites through in vitro challenges","description":"Supplementary Figure S1-5 and Tables S1-12 for manuscript entitled \"New insights into the Manila clam – Perkinsus olseni interaction based on gene expression analysis of clam hemocytes and parasite trophozoites through in vitro challenges\" by Hasanuzzaman A.F.M., Cao A., Ronza P., Fernández-Boo S., Rubiolo J.A., Robledo D., Gómez-Tato A., Álvarez-Dios J.A.,  Pardo B.G., Villalba A., Martínez P.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-11-12T13:31:43.914Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zvnw69chcz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zvnw69chcz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Abul Farah Md. ","last_name":"Hasanuzzaman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Asunción","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paolo","last_name":"Ronza","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sergio","last_name":"Fernández-Boo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan Andrés","last_name":"Rubiolo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diego","last_name":"Robledo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonio","last_name":"Gómez-Tato","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose Antonio","last_name":"Alvarez-Dios","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Belen G.","last_name":"Pardo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonio","last_name":"Villalba","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paulino","last_name":"Martínez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gvh2vtg86r","title":"Deciphering the Signaling Network Landscape of Breast Cancer Improves Drug Sensitivity Prediction","description":"Although genetic and epigenetic abnormalities in breast cancer have been extensively studied, it remains difficult to identify those patients who will respond to particular therapies. This is due in part to our lack of understanding of how the variability of cellular signaling affects drug sensitivity. Here, we used mass cytometry to characterize the single-cell signaling landscapes of 62 breast cancer cell lines and five lines from healthy tissue. We quantified 34 markers in each cell line upon stimulation by the growth factor EGF in the presence or absence of five kinase inhibitors. These data – on more than 80 million single cells from 4,000 conditions – were used to fit mechanistic signaling network models that provide unprecedented insights into the biological principles of how cancer cells process information. Our dynamic single-cell-based models more accurately predicted drug sensitivity than static bulk measurements for drugs targeting the PI3K-MTOR signaling pathway. Finally, we identified genomic features associated with drug sensitivity by using signaling phenotypes as proxies, including a missense mutation in DDIT3 predictive of PI3K-inhibition sensitivity. This provides proof of principle that single-cell measurements and modeling could inform matching of patients with appropriate treatments in the future.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-19T22:52:17.757Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gvh2vtg86r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gvh2vtg86r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Tognetti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Attila","last_name":"Gabor","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mi","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Valentina ","last_name":"Cappelletti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Windhager","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Konstantina","last_name":"Charmpi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natalie","last_name":"de Souza","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Beyer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paola","last_name":"Picotti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julio","last_name":"Saez-Rodriguez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bernd","last_name":"Bodenmiller","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Sensitivity Analysis","Cellular Signaling"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hvfhwjtr7n","title":"TGM2-H3Q5ser-NNMT Axis Licences Stromal-Mediated Cancerous Growth","description":"This work provides new insights into how serotonylation signaling is translated into chromatin-based regulation within the tumor microenvironment and expands our understanding of how emerging histone modifications shape tumor-associated stromal cell states. We believe that these findings will be of broad interest to researchers in cancer biology, epigenetics, and immunotherapy, as they provide new insights into how histone modifications shape tumor microenvironment remodeling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-08T17:27:28.131Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hvfhwjtr7n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hvfhwjtr7n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yu","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peiwei","last_name":"Chai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"prbgrt8n54","title":"Structural and mechanistic insights into the CAND1-mediated SCF substrate receptor exchange. Shaaban et al  ","description":"Structural and mechanistic insights into the CAND1-mediated SCF substrate receptor exchange. Shaaban et al","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-21T08:35:40.5Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/prbgrt8n54","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/prbgrt8n54"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Radoslav","last_name":"Enchev","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Biophysics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j2phm54bdm","title":"Mechanistic insights into successional plant-soil-microbe interactions in plateau pika-disturbed bare patches of alpine meadows","description":"Mechanistic insights into successional plant-soil-microbe interactions in plateau pika-disturbed bare patches of alpine meadows","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-30T17:46:55.622Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j2phm54bdm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j2phm54bdm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"媛媛","last_name":"段","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Alpine Ecosystem"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dvvxbf5v2p","title":"Mechanistic Insights into the Cis- and Trans-acting DNase Activities of Cas12a","description":"Raw imaging & fluorescence data belonging to the manuscript 'Structural Mechanistic Insights into Cis- and Trans-acting deoxyribonuclease activities of Cas12a' by Swarts.\n\nBoth raw image files and unprocessed .tif image files are uploaded.\n\nThe .pzfx files are GraphPad Prism files used to generate graphs based on gel scans and band signal intensity with the same name. Please not that trans-activity assays do not also contain images of gels as generated fluorescence was measured directly as described in the STAR methods.  \n\nFor (raw) gel images used for Figure 5, please see the identical gels used for Figure S7 and S8. The excel file contains the raw Size Exclusion Chromatography absorption and fluorescence readouts. \n\nThe .PDB and .mtz files corresponding to the manuscript are uploaded at the PDB database.  \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-12-04T11:13:35.917Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dvvxbf5v2p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dvvxbf5v2p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daan","last_name":"Swarts","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Jinek","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fluorescence Spectrometry","Gel Electrophoresis","Size Exclusion Chromatography","Absorption"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"j4zssvyf98","title":"Mechanistic Insights into GPCR-Gαβγ Complex Initiating Nucleotide Exchange","description":"Raw data for Figure 1B\nRaw data for Figures 2A-2G\nUncut Native-PAGE for Figure S2B\nUncut SDS-PAGE for FPLC fractions","source":{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-01T18:44:35.002Z","url":"https://digitalcommonsdata.usf.edu/datasets/j4zssvyf98","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j4zssvyf98"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Libin","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemical Analysis","Sodium Dodecyl Sulfate Polyacrylamide Gel Electrophoresis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n9g5x3h2cx","title":"Cell Metabolism-D-18-00502R-Unprocessed raw immunoblots","description":"This data include the unprocessed raw immunoblots for Cell Metabolism-D-18-00502R titled as \"Transmembrane 4 L six family member 5 senses arginine for mTORC1 signaling\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-15T12:48:18.548Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n9g5x3h2cx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n9g5x3h2cx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jung Weon","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139242,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":129,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139242,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138482,"restricted":70,"embargoed":690},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2293},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1466},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":703},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":470},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":313},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136591},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1903},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc